  "_id": "6a7f5797757ee51246381c5b",
  "lab": "6a7f5796eda1a5feaff0b458",
  "name": "Beta-2-Microglobulin",
  "code": "B2M",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<table><thead><tr><th><div>B2M Level (mg/L or µg/mL)</div></th><th><div>Interpretation</div></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><div>Normal Levels</div></td><td><div>Normal B2M levels in the blood are typically less than 2.5 mg/L (or 2.5 µg/mL), depending on the laboratory's reference range. Normal B2M levels in the urine are typically less than 0.3 mg/L (or 0.3 µg/mL). Normal B2M levels indicate normal kidney function and no significant abnormalities in protein metabolism.</div></td></tr><tr><td><div>Elevated Levels</div></td><td><div>Elevated B2M levels in the blood or urine may indicate kidney dysfunction or damage, such as in chronic kidney disease or acute kidney injury. Elevated B2M levels may also be seen in certain kidney disorders, such as multiple myeloma or lymphoma affecting the kidneys. Elevated B2M levels can also be associated with other conditions affecting the immune system. Further evaluation and testing are required to determine the underlying cause of elevated B2M levels.</div></td></tr><tr><td><div>Monitoring Cancer Treatment</div></td><td><div>In some cases, the B2M test may be used to monitor the response to cancer treatment, particularly in individuals with multiple myeloma or lymphoma. A decrease in B2M levels over time may indicate a positive response to treatment, while increasing or persistently high levels may indicate disease progression or treatment resistance.</div></td></tr></tbody></table>",
